@page "/learn/displayname-component"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>DisplayName · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">DisplayName</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Your form's model already names every field with <code>[Display]</code>. Why does the grid type them again?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Forms · Any render mode · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> a <code>&lt;DisplayName For="..." /&gt;</code> component that shows a
        property's <code>[Display]</code> name as plain text. In .NET 10, validation messages already used
        <code>[Display]</code>, but Blazor had no way to show the name on the page. MVC did this with
        <code>@@Html.DisplayNameFor</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a <i>Suppliers</i> grid. Its columns match the fields of the <i>New supplier</i> form. That form's
        model names each field with <code>[Display]</code>, in English and German.
    </p>
    <p>
        The grid's headers are done the .NET 10 way: two have a typed <code>Title</code>, and one has none. The
        model calls that third field <b>Main contact</b>, or <b>Hauptansprechpartner</b> in German.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The grid, with typed headers" Url="demo/displayname/suppliers/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: headers typed as Title" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            The third header shows the property name, <b>Contact</b>, not the model's Main contact. (The grid prints
            headers in capitals.) Click <b>Deutsch</b> at the top of the frame. Every header stays in English.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>Typed headers don't follow the model</h2>
    <p>The name is defined once, on the model, but the grid doesn't use it:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Where each header comes from</span>
        <pre class="snip">SupplierForm.Code     <span class="dim">[Display] Supplier code / Lieferantencode</span>
  └─ header: Title="Supplier code"     <span class="err">typed again, English only</span>
SupplierForm.Company  <span class="dim">[Display] Company name / Firmenname</span>
  └─ header: Title="Company name"      <span class="err">typed again, English only</span>
SupplierForm.Contact  <span class="dim">[Display] Main contact / Hauptansprechpartner</span>
  └─ header: no Title                  <span class="err">"Contact", the property name</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The model's <code>[Display]</code> names use <code>ResourceType</code> (see Fig. 4), so they come out in
        the current request's language. A typed <code>Title</code> is fixed text. If you rename or translate a field on the model, the grid
        keeps the old words. Leave <code>Title</code> out and QuickGrid shows the raw property name.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Point each header at the model</h2>
    <p>Put a <code>&lt;DisplayName For&gt;</code> in each column's <code>HeaderTemplate</code>.</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix, for one column</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;PropertyColumn Property="s =&gt; s.Contact"&gt;
    &lt;HeaderTemplate&gt;<span class="hl">&lt;DisplayName For="() =&gt; form.Contact" /&gt;</span>&lt;/HeaderTemplate&gt;
&lt;/PropertyColumn&gt;

<span class="hl">SupplierForm form = null!;</span>   <span class="dim">// never dereferenced</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        <b>Why <code>HeaderTemplate</code> and not <code>Title</code>?</b> <code>Title</code> takes a string,
        and <code>DisplayName</code> is a component, so it can't go there. .NET 11 doesn't add a method that
        returns the name as a string. If you'd rather keep <code>Title</code>, you still need your own helper, as
        in the .NET 10 way below.
    </p>
    <p>
        <b>Why point at the form's model?</b> The <code>[Display]</code> names are on <code>SupplierForm</code>. The
        grid's rows are <code>Supplier</code> entities, which have no names, so pointing at <code>Supplier</code>
        would show "Contact" again. A <code>HeaderTemplate</code> doesn't give you a row anyway, so the page
        keeps a <code>SupplierForm</code> field for <code>For</code> to point at. <code>For</code> only checks which
        property you named. It never reads the field's value, so the field can stay <code>null</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>DisplayName</code> reads <code>[Display]</code> each time it renders, so it always shows the current
        language.
    </p>
    <p>
        <b>The catch: a <code>HeaderTemplate</code> removes QuickGrid's sort button.</b> On a sortable column, add
        the button back inside the template, as below. Don't switch back to a typed <code>Title</code> to keep
        sorting: the header would stop following the model again. (Sorting needs an interactive render mode; the demos here are static and don't sort.)
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">A sortable column with DisplayName</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;PropertyColumn Property="s =&gt; s.Contact" Sortable="true"&gt;
    &lt;HeaderTemplate&gt;
        <span class="hl">&lt;button @@onclick="() =&gt; context.Grid.SortByColumnAsync(context)"&gt;</span>
            &lt;DisplayName For="() =&gt; form.Contact" /&gt;
        <span class="hl">&lt;/button&gt;</span>
    &lt;/HeaderTemplate&gt;
&lt;/PropertyColumn&gt;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>Here's the same grid with the fix on every column.</p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same grid, with DisplayName" Url="demo/displayname/suppliers"
                Label=".NET 11: DisplayName">
        <Caption>
            The third header now says <b>Main contact</b>, the model's name. Click <b>Deutsch</b> at the top of
            the frame: the headers switch to German. The German comes from the model's resource file, not the grid.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here's the page behind Fig. 2, the model it points at, and the page behind Fig. 1:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/DisplayNames/Suppliers.razor" Feature="displayname-component">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's grid. Each header points at <code>SupplierForm</code>, the form's model, not the grid's row type.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/DisplayNames/SupplierForm.cs" Feature="displayname-component">
        <Caption>
            Not new: the model, where each name is set once. <code>ResourceType</code> makes each name a key that's
            looked up in the request's language.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/DisplayNames/SuppliersNet10.razor" Feature="displayname-component">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 1's grid: two names typed again as <code>Title</code>, and one missing.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>A second copy, or your own helper</h2>
    <p>Fig. 1 shows a typed <code>Title</code>. Here are the other options in .NET 10:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Column headers in .NET 10</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;PropertyColumn Property="s =&gt; s.Contact"
    Title="@@L["MainContact"]" /&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why"><code>IStringLocalizer</code> looks the key up per request</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ translates, but you now keep the name in two places: rename it on the model and the header keeps the old name</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">Title="@@DisplayNames.For(() =&gt; form.Contact)"</pre>
                <span class="why">a helper you write that reads the property's <code>DisplayAttribute</code></span>
                <div class="verdict ok">✓ works, and keeps sorting; one more helper to write, test and copy between projects</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;DisplayName For="..." /&gt;</pre>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ warning RZ10012, "unexpected name 'DisplayName'": an empty, unknown element, so the headers are blank</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>A component that shows the name</h2>
    <p>
        <code>&lt;DisplayName For&gt;</code> is like <code>&lt;Label For&gt;</code>, but text only. It's Blazor's
        version of MVC's <code>DisplayNameFor</code>. It renders just the name, with no surrounding element, so it
        fits in a header, a table cell or a sentence. That's what Fig. 2 runs.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>It reads attributes, not <code>IStringLocalizer</code>.</b> If you translate with <code>IStringLocalizer</code> keys, like <code>Title="@@L[...]"</code> above, <code>DisplayName</code> won't see them. Move the names to <code>[Display(ResourceType = ...)]</code>, as this model does.</li>
        <li><b>Works in any render mode.</b> These demos are rendered once on the server, with no live connection. <code>DisplayName</code> shows the same text on an interactive page.</li>
        <li><b>Fallback order:</b> <code>[Display(Name)]</code>, then <code>[DisplayName]</code>, then the property name.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
